                    <span>{item.number}</span>
                    {item.title}
                  </a>
                ))}
              </nav>
            </details>

            <!-- ---------- THESIS ---------- -->
            <section class="enamel-thesis" aria-label="The short answer">
              <span class="enamel-thesis__label">The short answer</span>
              <p>
                Soft enamel leaves the metal lines standing proud; hard enamel
                grinds them flush and polishes the surface flat. Neither is the
                better material. Soft enamel is the more forgiving surface for
                line-heavy artwork and the cheaper one to produce. Hard enamel
                is the more expensive, more uniform surface — the one that reads
                as a finished object rather than a printed badge. The decision
                usually turns on a single question: is this piece handled in
                bulk, or sold one at a time?
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 01 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="naming"
              class="enamel-section"
              data-article-section="naming"
            >
              <header class="enamel-section__header">
                <span class="enamel-section__index">01</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="enamel-section__eyebrow">Terminology</p>
                  <h2>Two names, one confusing vocabulary</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Both names start from the same object: a metal body with
                recessed cells, formed by a stamping die or a die-cast mould.
                The cells are the artwork. Everything that separates the two
                methods happens after the color goes in.
              </p>

              <p>
                Soft enamel — 烤漆 in most Chinese supply chains — is filled and
                cured, and that is the end of the surface work. Hard enamel,
                仿珐琅, is filled, baked, ground flat against the metal lines,
                polished, and then plated. The word “hard” describes the
                geometry of the finished face, not the hardness of the enamel
                itself: a cured soft enamel layer is hard too. What changes is
                that one surface has relief and the other does not.
              </p>

              <p>
                仿珐琅 translates literally as “imitation cloisonné”, and that is
                the honest description. True cloisonné builds walls from wire,
                fills them with glass frit, and fires the piece repeatedly at
                high temperature before polishing it with stone. Imitation hard
                enamel reproduces the look with stamped metal walls and
                synthetic enamel. At arm's length the two are close. In
                durability, in labour, and in price, they are not the same
                craft — so a quotation that says “cloisonné” should be read as
                “hard enamel” until the process is confirmed.
              </p>

              <div class="enamel-note">
                <p class="enamel-note__label">Three questions that replace the label</p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Is the color flush with the metal lines?</li>
                  <li>Was the face ground and polished after filling?</li>
                  <li>Was the piece plated after polishing?</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="enamel-note__foot">
                  Three yes answers mean hard enamel, whatever the quotation
                  calls it. A no on any of them means you are buying a polished
                  front on a soft enamel part.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 02 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="cross-section"
              class="enamel-section"
              data-article-section="cross-section"
            >
              <header class="enamel-section__header">
                <span class="enamel-section__index">02</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="enamel-section__eyebrow">Process</p>
                  <h2>The cross-section explains the rest</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                A pin body is usually 1.5–3.0 mm thick, with cells cut 0.3–0.5 mm
                deep. The metal left standing between the cells is the line work
                you see in the artwork — usually 0.25–0.4 mm wide at the die.
                That ridge is the whole story.
              </p>

              <p>
                In soft enamel, the color is deposited into the cells and cured.
                Surface tension and cure shrinkage pull the fill slightly below
                the crest, leaving a recess of roughly 0.05–0.15 mm. The piece
                is then plated, and the plating follows the relief — which is why
                the lines stay bright and the color stays in shadow.
              </p>

              <p>
                In hard enamel, the cells are overfilled and baked, then the
                entire face is ground down on a wet abrasive belt until the metal
                crest is exposed. The surface is polished with compound, and the
                piece is plated afterwards — because grinding cuts through the
                first plating layer. Some shops plate before grinding as well, so
                the metal does not oxidise while it is being worked.
              </p>

              <figure class="enamel-figure">
                <div class="enamel-figure__frame">
                  <svg
                    viewBox="0 0 660 236"
                    role="img"
                    aria-labelledby="xsec-title xsec-desc"
