                      Cross-section of soft enamel and hard enamel
                    </title>
                    <desc id="xsec-desc">
                      Soft enamel leaves the metal ridges raised above the color;
                      hard enamel is ground so the color sits flush with the
                      metal lines.
                    </desc>

                    <path
                      d="M20 196 L20 132 L60 132 L60 106 L96 106 L96 132 L244 132 L244 106 L280 106 L280 132 L320 132 L320 196 Z"
                      fill="var(--color-paper)"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></path>
                    <rect
                      x="96"
                      y="120"
                      width="148"
                      height="12"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.45"
                    ></rect>
                    <line
                      x1="110"
                      y1="106"
                      x2="110"
                      y2="120"
                      stroke="var(--color-ink)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <text
                      x="118"
                      y="112"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                    >
                      0.05–0.15 mm
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="78"
                      y="99"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      ridge
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="170"
                      y="152"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      enamel sits below
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="170"
                      y="224"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      font-weight="700"
                      letter-spacing="0.12em"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      SOFT ENAMEL
                    </text>

                    <rect
                      x="340"
                      y="132"
                      width="300"
                      height="64"
                      fill="var(--color-paper)"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="1"
                    ></rect>
                    <rect
                      x="340"
                      y="132"
                      width="300"
                      height="14"
                      fill="var(--color-gold)"
                      opacity="0.45"
                    ></rect>
                    <rect
                      x="380"
                      y="132"
                      width="10"
                      height="14"
                      fill="var(--color-paper)"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></rect>
                    <rect
                      x="540"
                      y="132"
                      width="10"
                      height="14"
                      fill="var(--color-paper)"
                      stroke="var(--color-line)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></rect>
                    <line
                      x1="404"
                      y1="132"
                      x2="524"
                      y2="132"
                      stroke="var(--color-ink)"
                      stroke-width="0.75"
                    ></line>
                    <text
                      x="464"
                      y="124"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      flush — 0 mm
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="385"
                      y="160"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      fill="var(--color-muted-2)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      metal line
                    </text>
                    <text
                      x="490"
                      y="224"
                      font-family="var(--font-body)"
                      font-size="10"
                      font-weight="700"
                      letter-spacing="0.12em"
                      fill="var(--color-ink)"
                      text-anchor="middle"
                    >
                      HARD ENAMEL
                    </text>
                  </svg>
                </div>
                <figcaption>
                  Schematic cross-section, not to scale. The only structural
                  difference between the two products is the height of the
                  enamel relative to the metal line.
                </figcaption>
              </figure>

              <p>
                Because grinding removes material, a hard enamel body needs
                enough wall thickness and enough fill depth to survive it. The
                finished piece is usually a little heavier, and the metal lines
                are a little thinner at the crest than they were on the die.
                Every consequence on this page — how it feels, which artwork
                survives, how it wears, what it costs — comes from that one
                extra operation.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 03 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="surface"
              class="enamel-section"
              data-article-section="surface"
            >
              <header class="enamel-section__header">
                <span class="enamel-section__index">03</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="enamel-section__eyebrow">Perception</p>
                  <h2>What you see and what you feel</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Tilt a soft enamel piece under a desk lamp and every metal line
                throws its own thin shadow. The artwork reads as carved, because
                the surface is genuinely carved. Hard enamel under the same lamp
                returns one continuous reflection: the metal lines read as drawn
                lines, not as walls, and the design becomes a set of color fields
                bounded by hairlines.
              </p>

              <p>
                In the hand the difference is just as literal. Soft enamel feels
                like letterpress — a fine series of ridges you can trace with a
                fingernail. Hard enamel feels like a glass tile: one plane, no
                texture, slightly cooler and denser.
              </p>

              <p>
                Color behaves differently too, and this is where most approvals
                go wrong. A matte soft enamel field scatters light, so a Pantone
                match reads lighter and flatter on the finished pin than on the
                chip. A polished hard enamel field reflects its surroundings, so
                the color reads deeper and more even — and in product photography
                it will pick up whatever is in front of it. Judge both on the
                finished surface under D65, never on a screen.
              </p>

              <div class="enamel-table-wrap">
                <table class="enamel-table">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Surface</th>
                      <th scope="col">Under light</th>
                      <th scope="col">Color</th>
                      <th scope="col">How it ages</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {surfaceRows.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <td>{row.name}</td>
                        <td>{row.light}</td>
                        <td>{row.color}</td>
                        <td>{row.aging}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p>
                There is a third option that buyers often discover too late.
                A soft enamel piece can be finished with an epoxy dome: a resin
                layer that fills the recesses and levels the face. It looks close
                to hard enamel at arm's length and costs far less, but it is a
                coating, not a ground surface. Domes yellow under UV, scratch
                with handling, and soften fine type — they suit printed artwork
                better than line art.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 04 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="limits"
              class="enamel-section"
              data-article-section="limits"
            >
              <header class="enamel-section__header">
                <span class="enamel-section__index">04</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="enamel-section__eyebrow">Artwork</p>
                  <h2>Where the artwork breaks</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                Both methods share the same hard limit: the design has to exist
                as closed cells of solid color separated by metal. What differs
                is how much of that design survives the finishing step. The
                numbers below are typical shop ranges rather than standards —
                treat them as the point where you should ask, not as a rule.
              </p>

              <div class="enamel-table-wrap">
                <table class="enamel-table enamel-table--limits">
                  <thead>
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Constraint</th>
                      <th scope="col">Soft</th>
                      <th scope="col">Hard</th>
                      <th scope="col">Why</th>
                    </tr>
                  </thead>
                  <tbody>
                    {limitRows.map((row) => (
                      <tr>
                        <td>{row.limit}</td>
                        <td>{row.soft}</td>
                        <td>{row.hard}</td>
                        <td>{row.why}</td>
                      </tr>
                    ))}
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>

              <p>
                The constraint that surprises most buyers is text. Reversed-out
                lettering — metal letters inside a color field — depends on a
                metal stroke surviving the finish. In soft enamel a 1.2 mm stroke
                is comfortable. In hard enamel the same stroke is ground and
                polished from the top down, so the letter loses weight and any
                serif detail softens into a suggestion. If the design depends on
                small type, it is a soft enamel design, and forcing it into hard
                enamel produces a pin that looks like a bad print.
              </p>

              <p>
                Gradients and halftones are a separate problem: neither method
                can hold them, because enamel is a solid color in a cell. Those
                designs belong to offset or UV printing, with or without a dome.
                A halftone can be simulated with a grid of tiny cells, but the
                cells have a minimum size, so the dot pattern becomes visible and
                the cost climbs with every separation.
              </p>

              <p>
                The working rule: if the design depends on thin lines, texture
                and layered relief, it was drawn for soft enamel. If it depends
                on silhouette, color blocking and a clean edge, it was drawn for
                hard enamel. Redrawing artwork for the wrong process costs more
                than the price difference between the two.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 05 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="failure"
              class="enamel-section"
              data-article-section="failure"
            >
              <header class="enamel-section__header">
                <span class="enamel-section__index">05</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="enamel-section__eyebrow">Durability</p>
                  <h2>What actually fails first</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                “More durable” is the phrase most often used to sell hard enamel,
                and it is only half true. The two surfaces fail in different
                places, at different moments, for different reasons. Which one
                lasts longer depends on how the piece is used, not on which
                process is nominally superior.
              </p>

              <p>
                One honest caveat before the detail: there is no industry-wide
                abrasion standard for decorative enamel fill. Durability claims
                about enamel are comparative, not certified. Plating is the part
                that can be tested — salt spray, abrasion cycles, nickel release
                — so when a supplier talks about durability, ask which test the
                number comes from.
              </p>

              <div class="enamel-failures">
                {failures.map((item) => (
                  <div class="enamel-failure">
                    <div class="enamel-failure__head">
                      <span class="enamel-failure__num">{item.num}</span>
                      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="enamel-failure__body">
                      <div>
                        <span class="enamel-failure__tag">Soft enamel</span>
                        <p>{item.soft}</p>
                      </div>
                      <div>
                        <span class="enamel-failure__tag">Hard enamel</span>
                        <p>{item.hard}</p>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                The practical reading: soft enamel protects its color by putting
                metal in the way, so it survives knocks and bulk handling well
                and dulls gradually. Hard enamel protects itself with a denser,
                flatter surface, so it resists abrasion and cleans up well, but
                it shows scratches and edge chips more openly. A hard enamel pin
                with thin plating will still fail at the plating, exactly like a
                soft enamel one.
              </p>

              <div class="enamel-note">
                <p class="enamel-note__label">Durability is a system</p>
                <p class="enamel-note__foot">
                  Enamel method, plating thickness, attachment, and packaging
                  determine survival together. Care is the same for both: dry
                  microfibre cloth, no ultrasonic bath, no solvent, no stacking
                  loose pins in a bag.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 06 ---------- -->
            <section id="cost" class="enamel-section" data-article-section="cost">
              <header class="enamel-section__header">
                <span class="enamel-section__index">06</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="enamel-section__eyebrow">Cost</p>
                  <h2>Where the price difference comes from</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                The tooling is the same. Both methods run off the same die or the
                same mould, so the mould fee does not change — that is a separate
                conversation, covered in
                <a href="/resources/metal-craft-guide#guide-forming"
                  >deep dive 02</a
                >. The difference is entirely in what happens to each piece after
                the color goes in.
              </p>

              <ul class="enamel-drivers">
                {drivers.map((item) => (
                  <li>
                    <span class="enamel-drivers__tag">{item.tag}</span>
                    <div>
                      <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                      <p>{item.text}</p>
                    </div>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ul>

              <p>
                In practice, hard enamel usually prices 15–35% above soft enamel
                at the unit level, for the same design and quantity. The low end
                of that range belongs to simple shapes with few colors at volume;
                the high end belongs to multi-color designs with large flat
                fields, where grinding and polishing have more surface to keep
                level and the reject rate climbs.
              </p>

              <p>
                The gap is labour-dominated, which means it does not behave like
                a material cost. Doubling the order does not halve the
                difference: the absolute gap per piece stays roughly flat, so the
                percentage narrows slowly while the money does not. If a quote
                shows hard enamel at soft enamel pricing, the more likely
                explanations are a polished front only, a domed print described
                as hard enamel, or a reject allowance quietly moved into the
                replacement terms.
              </p>

              <p>
                There is also a cost that never appears on the enamel line.
                Hard enamel sets an expectation: a piece finished to that surface
                is usually presented on a backing card or in a box, and that
                packaging can cost more than the upgrade itself. Budget the
                presentation with the process, not after it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 07 ---------- -->
            <section
              id="verify"
              class="enamel-section"
              data-article-section="verify"
            >
              <header class="enamel-section__header">
                <span class="enamel-section__index">07</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="enamel-section__eyebrow">Verification</p>
                  <h2>How to verify it on a sample</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                None of the above matters if the goods arrive as something else.
                Five checks settle the question in under a minute, and none of
                them require a lab.
              </p>

              <ol class="enamel-checks">
                {checks.map((item) => (
                  <li>
                    <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                    <p>{item.text}</p>
                  </li>
                ))}
              </ol>

              <p>
                One last distinction worth making explicit in the order: a golden
                sample from the tooling shop and a production sample from the
                running batch are not the same object. Ask for the second one
                before the bulk run, and keep the approved piece sealed as the
                reference for the inspection.
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- CLOSING ---------- -->
            <footer class="enamel-conclusion">
              <div class="enamel-conclusion__rule"></div>
              <p class="enamel-conclusion__label">The decision</p>

              <div class="enamel-rule">
                <p>
                  <strong>Handled in bulk, line-heavy artwork, fixed budget.</strong>
                  Soft enamel.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Sold as an object, color-blocked artwork, premium shelf.</strong>
                  Hard enamel.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <strong>Both in one program.</strong> Split the run — soft for
                  the giveaway tier, hard for the retail tier. It is the same
                  artwork and the same die.
                </p>
              </div>

              <p class="enamel-conclusion__text">
                If the brief is still open — a design that could go either way, a
                quantity that sits between two price breaks — send it over with
                the artwork. The answer is usually visible in the line work
                before it is visible in the quote.
              </p>

              <div class="enamel-conclusion__signature">
                <span>METALCRAFTS</span>
                <span>CRAFT · DETAIL · QUALITY</span>
              </div>

              {/*
                Cluster navigation rule:
                01 → left: back to the guide, right: next deep dive
                02–05 → left: previous deep dive, right: next deep dive
                06 → left: previous deep dive, right: back to the guide
              */}
              <nav class="enamel-cluster" aria-label="Guide navigation">
                <a
